WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.260
This episode was made possible by generous supporters on Patreon.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.760
Happy Pi Day, Crazies!

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.560
Well, at least if you live in one of these countries.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.660
Anyway, I was thinking recently:

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.900
Does the number pi actually exist?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.080
Let’s find out!

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.560
Of course, we can’t really answer this without a pinch of definition.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.780
The number pi is defined as the ratio of a circle’s circumference to its diameter.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.720
Yes, there are bunch of different ways to
calculate it, but as 3blue1brown would remind us:

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.600
All of them are somehow related to circles.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.740
So, if we’re going to ask if the number pi exists,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.300
it might be helpful to first ask if a circle exists.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.600
A circle is the set of all points equidistant from the same point.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.360
But that’s just math. It’s abstract stuff.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.960
Can we actually draw a shape with that property?

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.820
Well, let’s give it a shot.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.780
Find a piece of paper and a compass.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.660
The ends on the compass have a consistent distance, so it should be perfect.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.280
I can put it on my paper, twirl it around, and

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.800
BAM! I’ve got a circle.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.200
Or do I?

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.320
Let us take a closer look.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.040
You can quickly see places I was a bit wobbly with the compass.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.880
Sometimes it’s so bad my curve doesn’t even reconnect with itself.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.940
But, even if I didn’t suck at drawing, I’m still limited by the width of the line,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.620
the graphite material in the pencil, and imperfections in the paper.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.420
So, let’s try something manufactured a little better like this pan.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.980
If I measure the diameter all the way around, it seems consistent,

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.520
at least to the precision of my measuring device.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.760
Which brings us to our first problem with physical objects.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.700
If you measure the circumference and the diameter and then divide those two numbers,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.880
you’ll get something close to pi, but not exactly pi.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.540
You can’t know for sure that it’s a perfect circle.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.660
But what if it is a perfect circle? Then does pi exist?

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.380
Eh, not so fast.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.440
This brings us to our second problem with physical objects.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.640
Even if this pan is as round as physically possible, it’s still not a perfect circle.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:57.900
Super Zoom!

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.600
If you get close enough, you can see atoms.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.660
There are sides to this shape.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.200
They’re just really tiny ones and curved ones at that.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.220
Also, we’re now zoomed in so much that size and shape start to lose meaning.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.480
Even this digital circle I showed you earlier is made of pixels on your screen.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.400
If you put your eye ball right up against the screen, you’d see something like this.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.220
That’s not a smooth edge.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.180
So I don’t know if this whole circle thing is going to pan out.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.080
Anyway, all this talk about circles reminds
me of this experiment

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.000
that Physics Girl did a while back.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.460
She and Veritasium threw a bunch of darts at a board with circles drawn on it.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:41.160
The ratio of darts that landed inside the circles compared to the total should be proportional to pi.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.620
Eh, roughly.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.640
You know what?

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.240
I’m kind of in the mood to check the limits of that experiment.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.420
Computer simulation time!

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:54.420
Here is a board hit with 1000 darts, 10 thousand darts, 100 thousand, and a million.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:59.360
I ran each simulation 100 times and there’s a definite decrease in the average error

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.780
the more darts you have.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.940
Unfortunately, sometimes randomness comes in lumps.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.960
If I ran this simulation enough times, there would eventually be one

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.680
where all the darts landed inside the circle

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.580
and I’d get a little over 27% error.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:17.240
The point is, there will always be some error with this experiment.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.000
That being said, Dianna and Derek got really great results. You should go check it out.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.600
Hold up. I think I hear comments incoming.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.740
All computers do is work with numbers,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:31.300
so your simulation is assuming pi exist in order to prove pi exists.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.780
Check. Mate.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.380
Let’s make a few things clear.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.260
One!

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.300
I haven’t come to any conclusions, so you shouldn’t either.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.160
Two!

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.919
Oh, you want to play the pedantic game?

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.699
You’re going up against the master!

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.800
Technically speaking, computers don’t work with numbers.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.300
In a computer, there are a bunch of transistors controlling the flow of electricity.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.980
The electricity is either flowing or it isn’t.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.320
That’s the reality.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.520
We simply interpret a flow as a 1 and a lack
of flow as a 0,

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:03.340
because it allows us to interpret complex flows of electricity as binary math.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.000
There's no actual numbers there. It’s just electricity.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.880
But, you know what, let’s pretend they are numbers for a minute.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.980
Even if we do that, pi is not one of those numbers.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:18.160
A typical processor these days is 64-bit, which means each register is 64 bits long.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.740
Each bit can either be a zero or a one, so
we’re working with binary math.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.860
Unfortunately, that doesn’t mean we get up to 64 binary digits.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.740
We’d like to work with negative numbers as well as positive ones.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:33.020
Also, like most numbers, pi has decimal places.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.520
Remember, it looks like this in decimal form.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.980
There’s a decimal point.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:39.260
In binary, it looks like this.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.480
There’s still a point, but this is binary,
so we call it a binary point instead.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:49.740
A computer needs to handle numbers like this, so we let the point float around the 64-bit register.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.880
That’s why it’s called a floating point number.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:56.680
OK, so we sacrifice one bit is for the sign, then 11 more bits for an exponent.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:01.020
The actual digits of the number only get the remaining 52 bits.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.740
In binary scientific notation, a number looks like this.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.860
So, the best a computer can do with pi is this:

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.000
Converting that only gets us 18 decimal places.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.040
What if it’s, like, a super mega advanced processor?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.960
There will still be a limit.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.480
They do make highly specialized 256-bit processors, which means more digits,

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.100
but there will always be a finite number of them.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.300
The number pi has an infinite number of digits.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.380
Computers can only ever store an approximation of pi, not pi itself.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.920
We don’t call it an irrational number for nothing.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:39.080
I mean, there is no discernible pattern whatsoever in those digits.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.320
That’s crazy, right?!

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.740
Actually, this might be a better way to handle our original question.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.220
Does the number pi actually exist?

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.760
Let’s imagine that all numbers are on this
hypothetical line.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:53.940
It’s got all the natural numbers: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, etc.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.040
Zero is on there too as well as all the negative integers.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.800
It’s also pretty easy to imagine rational numbers.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.820
You just make ratios of the integers:

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.960
1/2, -33/4, 10/3, -1/12,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.100
each with a corresponding decimal version that either ends or repeats.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:15.000
Irrational numbers are bit more difficult.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.700
More ratios just gives you more rational numbers.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.620
But, let’s say you’ve got a triangle with
two equal sides.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.160
We’ll give them a length of one.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:23.880
That’s no big deal. One is an integer.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:25.480
We’re pretty comfortable with that.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:29.300
Unfortunately, the length of the remaining side is root two.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.000
Root two is irrational.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.400
You can’t write it as a ratio of integers.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.740
Its decimal places go on forever with no pattern.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.400
There are lots of other root examples, but
pi is even weirder.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.880
It’s transcendental, meaning it isn’t the solution to any algebraic equation

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.400
with rational coefficients.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.440
It transcends algebra.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:53.920
But numbers like root two and pi have to be on the number line if it’s going to be continuous.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:56.160
Without them, there would be gaps.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.600
For that reason, we call this line the real number line

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.980
because it contains all real numbers.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.060
So the real question here is this:

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.900
Do space and time work like the number line? Is spacetime continuous?

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.480
Well, general relativity would have you think so.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.920
It models space as being like the real number line in all 3 dimensions

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.180
and also models time the same way.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.220
But this fact about general relativity is what makes it

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.040
fundamentally inconsistent with quantum mechanics.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.500
For gravity to work like the other interactions in quantum mechanics,

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.700
space and time must be discrete.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.560
They must come in little pieces.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.080
That’s the exact opposite of continuous.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.240
In a model like that, having gaps in the number line would be perfectly acceptable.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.720
It might be that no irrational numbers exist, let alone pi.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.000
Unfortunately, we don’t have a model like
this yet, at least one that works.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.500
Until then, we won’t a definitive answer.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.480
So what do you think?

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.760
Are space and time continuous or discrete?

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:51.960
Does pi exist?

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.460
Duke it out in the comments.

